    explanation: "Articles are tiny words, but they are VERY important. Think of them like traffic signs for nouns. They tell us if we are talking about ONE specific thing, ANY one thing, or NO specific thing at all.\n\nWHAT IS A COUNTABLE NOUN?\nA countable noun is something you can count with your fingers: one apple, two cats, three books. If you can add 's' to make it plural, it is countable. Most nouns in English are countable.\n\nWHAT IS AN UNCOUNTABLE NOUN?\nAn uncountable noun is something you cannot count individually. You cannot say 'one water' or 'two informations'. Examples: water, rice, milk, air, music, love, information.\n\nWHAT IS A VOWEL SOUND?\nA vowel sound is the sound your mouth makes when it is open. The five vowel letters are: a, e, i, o, u. But here is the tricky part: we care about the SOUND, not the letter. University starts with u but sounds like you, which is a consonant sound. So we say a university, not an university.\n\nWHAT IS A CONSONANT SOUND?\nA consonant sound happens when your mouth is more closed. Examples: b, c, d, f, g, h, j, k, l, m, n, p, q, r, s, t, v, w, x, y, z.\n\nTHE THREE MAGIC WORDS:\n1. A / AN = ANY one thing (not a specific one)\n   - Use a before consonant sounds: a book, a cat, a university\n   - Use an before vowel sounds: an apple, an egg, an hour\n\n2. THE = a SPECIFIC thing (both people know which one)\n   - The apple was delicious - we know which apple\n   - The sun is hot - there is only one sun\n\n3. NO ARTICLE = general ideas or uncountable nouns\n   - Water is important - general idea, uncountable\n   - Dogs are friendly - general plural\n\nSIMPLE STEPS:\n1. Is the noun countable and singular? YES -> step 2. NO -> no article.\n2. Is it specific? YES -> the. NO -> step 3.\n3. Does it start with a vowel SOUND? YES -> an. NO -> a.\n\nREMEMBER: Say the word out loud. If the first sound is a, e, i, o, u, use an. Otherwise, use a.",
