    summary: "Connect ideas with because, when, if, and although.",
    summaryZh: "用 because, when, if, although 连接想法。",
    explanation: "English sentences are built like Lego blocks. You start with one block and add more pieces to make it bigger and more interesting.\n\nTHE BASIC BLOCK: SVO\nS = Subject (who or what)\nV = Verb (action)\nO = Object (what receives the action)\n\nExample: I ate pizza.\n- I = Subject\n- ate = Verb\n- pizza = Object\n\nThis is the smallest complete English sentence. You can understand it. It has a beginning, middle, and end.\n\nADDING DETAILS:\nYou can add details to make the sentence longer:\n1. Add an adjective: I ate a DELICIOUS pizza.\n2. Add another adjective: I ate a delicious PEPPERONI pizza.\n3. Add a place: I ate a delicious pepperoni pizza AT MARIO'S.\n4. Add a time: I ate a delicious pepperoni pizza at Mario's LAST NIGHT.\n\nSee? We built a long sentence by adding one piece at a time. Each piece is optional.\n\nCONNECTING SENTENCES WITH CLAUSES:\nSometimes we want to connect two ideas. We use clause connectors:\n- BECAUSE = reason\n  I stayed home BECAUSE it rained.\n  (Why did I stay home? Because it rained.)\n\n- WHEN = time\n  I'll call you WHEN I arrive.\n  (When will I call? When I arrive.)\n\n- IF = condition\n  IF it rains, we will stay home.\n  (What if it rains? We will stay home.)\n\n- ALTHOUGH = contrast\n  ALTHOUGH it rained, I went out.\n  (Even though it rained, I still went out.)\n\nIMPORTANT: Each clause needs its own subject and verb!\n- Because it rained - subject = it, verb = rained. GOOD!\n- Because raining - NO subject, NO verb. BAD!\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\n- Running on without periods: I went to the store which is on Main Street where I bought milk that was cheap because...\n  FIX: Break into smaller sentences. I went to the store. It is on Main Street. I bought milk. It was cheap.\n- Forgetting the subject in clauses: Because is raining - WRONG! Because it is raining.\n\nBUILDING TIP:\nAlways ask: Does every sentence have a subject + verb?\nIf yes -> good!\nIf no -> add a subject.",
    explanationZh: "从句为主句添加额外信息。'because' 表示原因，'when' 表示时间，'if' 表示条件，'although' 表示对比。每个从句都需要自己的主语和动词。",
    examples: [
      { en: "I stayed home because it rained.", zh: "我待在家里因为下雨了。", explanation: "'Because' introduces a reason clause. The clause 'because it rained' needs its own subject (it) and verb (rained)." },
      { en: "Although it rained, I went out.", zh: "虽然下雨了，我还是出去了。", explanation: "'Although' shows contrast. The main clause and the although-clause can be in either order. Notice the comma when 'although' starts the sentence." },
      { en: "I'll call you when I arrive.", zh: "我到了就给你打电话。", explanation: "'When' connects two events in time. The future tense is not needed after 'when'; we use present simple ('arrive') to refer to the future." },
      { en: "If it rains, I will stay home.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "If + condition, will for result" },
      { en: "I know that you are right.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "That you are right is noun clause" },
      { en: "She smiled when she saw me.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "When she saw me is time clause" },
      { en: "Although it rained, I went out.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Although shows contrast" },
      { en: "I will call you when I arrive.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "When clauses use present simple" },
      { en: "Because it was late, we went home.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Because gives reason" },
      { en: "The book that you gave me is interesting.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "That you gave me describes book" }
    ],
