    explanation: "Modal verbs are special helper verbs. They change the meaning of the main verb and add feelings like ability, permission, advice, or obligation.\n\nThe most common modals are: can, could, should, must, might, may, would.\n\nWHAT IS ABILITY?\nAbility means 'can I do it?' or 'can I not do it?'\n- I can swim = I have the ability to swim.\n- I cannot fly = I do not have the ability to fly.\n\nWHAT IS PERMISSION?\nPermission means 'am I allowed to do it?'\n- Can I go now? = Am I allowed to leave?\n- Could I borrow your pen? = May I please use your pen?\n\nWHAT IS OBLIGATION?\nObligation means 'I must do it' or 'I have to do it.'\n- You must wear a seatbelt = It is the rule. You have no choice.\n- You should study = It is a good idea, but you can choose.\n\nWHAT IS ADVICE?\nAdvice means 'I think you should do it because it is good for you.'\n- You should see a doctor = It would be a good idea.\n- You had better hurry = If you do not hurry, there will be a problem.\n\nWHAT IS POSSIBILITY?\nPossibility means 'maybe it will happen.'\n- It might rain = There is a chance of rain.\n- It may rain = Similar to might, but slightly more likely.\n\nTHE BASIC RULE:\nAfter a modal verb, ALWAYS use the base verb (the dictionary form). NO to, NO -s, NO -ed.\n\nCORRECT: Can you help me?\nWRONG: Can you to help me?\nWRONG: Can you helps me?\n\nMODAL STRENGTH LADDER:\nFrom strongest to weakest:\n1. MUST = I have to do this (no choice)\n2. SHOULD = This is a good idea (you can choose)\n3. COULD = This is one option (you can choose)\n4. MIGHT = Maybe this will happen (not sure)\n\nExample:\nYou MUST stop (It is the law!)\nYou SHOULD stop (It is a good idea)\nYou COULD stop (It is one choice)\nYou MIGHT stop (Maybe you will, maybe you won't)\n\nCOMMON MISTAKES:\n- Can you to help me? - WRONG! No to after modals.\n- I must to go - WRONG! Must go is correct.\n- He can swims - WRONG! No -s after modals. He can swim.\n\nDEDUCTION (GUESSING):\n- It must be John = I am 100% sure.\n- It might be John = Maybe 50% sure.\n- It can't be John = I am sure it is NOT John.",
    explanationZh: "'Will' 常用于说话时的即时决定。'Going to' 用于计划或有证据表明某事会发生。两者在将来预测中常可互换。",
    examples: [
      { en: "I'll answer the phone. (spontaneous)", zh: "我去接电话。（即时决定）", explanation: "'Will' is often used for decisions made at the moment of speaking. You did not plan to answer it; you decided right now." },
      { en: "I'm going to study medicine. (plan)", zh: "我打算学医。（计划）", explanation: "'Going to' is used for plans or intentions that existed before the moment of speaking. You thought about it earlier." },
      { en: "Look out! The car is coming!", zh: "小心！车来了！", explanation: "Here 'will' is NOT used for a plan—it is a spontaneous prediction based on what you see right now." },
      { en: "I will help you with that.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Will for spontaneous decisions" },
      { en: "I am going to visit Japan next year.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Going to for pre-made plans" },
      { en: "It will rain tonight.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Will for predictions" },
      { en: "Look out! That cup is going to fall.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Going to for predictions based on evidence" },
      { en: "I will call you when I arrive.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Will for promises" },
      { en: "She is going to start a new job.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Going to for existing intentions" },
      { en: "Do not worry. I will be there.", zh: "见英文解释。", explanation: "Will for promises" }
    ],
